A. LEXICAL SPECIFICATION

A.1 SOURCE ENCODING
  UTF-8 only. BOM (0xEF 0xBB 0xBF) is stripped by the lexer.
  Line endings: \n (Unix) or \r\n (Windows). \r alone is an error.

A.2 COMMENTS
  Line comment: // extends to end of line
  Block comment: /* ... */ (nestable to depth 16)

A.3 WHITESPACE
  Space (0x20), Tab (0x09), Newline (0x0A), Carriage Return (0x0D),
  Form Feed (0x0C), Vertical Tab (0x0B).

A.4 TOKENS

A.4.1 Keywords (30 — Frozen, case-sensitive)

  Keyword    Category
  ───        ──────────
  1  module   Module
  2  import   Module
  3  fn       Function
  4  pub      Visibility
  5  const    Declaration
  6  var      Declaration
  7  comptime Compile-time
  8  defer    Cleanup
  9  errdefer Error cleanup
  10 test     Testing
  11 return   Control flow
  12 if       Control flow
  13 else     Control flow
  14 while    Loop
  15 for      Loop
  16 try      Error handling
  17 break    Control flow
  18 continue Control flow
  19 match    Control flow
  20 struct   Type
  21 union    Type
  22 enum     Type
  23 traits   Type system
  24 extend   Type system
  25 type     Type system
  26 unsafe   Safety
  27 noreturn Type / control
  28 as       Conversion
  29 with     Syntax / context
  30 undefined Value

A.4.2 Built-in Primitive Types (Reserved, NOT Keywords)

  Signed integers:   i8, i16, i32, i64, i128, isize
  Unsigned integers: u8, u16, u32, u64, u128, usize
  Floating point:    f32, f64
  Other:             bool, void

  Bitfield widths (valid ONLY inside #[packed] struct fields):
    u1, u2, u3, u4, u5, u6, u7

  Primitive type names are reserved and cannot be shadowed by user
  declarations, but they are NOT classified as keywords.
  They are valid only in type-expression context.
  There is NO char primitive type. Character literals (A.4.9) yield u8.
  There is NO string primitive type. String data is represented as
  []const u8 or *const [N:0]u8. Growable string types live in std lib.

A.4.3 Reserved Literal Tokens (NOT Keywords)

  Boolean: true | false
  Null:    null

  These are recognized by the lexer as dedicated literal tokens.
  They are reserved and cannot be used as identifiers.

A.4.4 Undefined Literal
  undefined

  undefined IS a keyword (see A.4.1 #30).
  Represents uninitialized memory (see C.9).

A.4.5 Identifiers
  identifier ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
  Must not match any keyword, primitive type name, or reserved literal.
  Identifiers beginning with __ (double underscore) are reserved for
  compiler-generated symbols.

A.4.6 Contextual Identifiers

  self — method receiver. Lexes as IDENTIFIER; gains special meaning
         during semantic analysis inside struct/union/enum/trait methods.

  Self — current enclosing type. Lexes as IDENTIFIER; gains special meaning
         during semantic analysis as a type alias to the enclosing type.

  Neither self nor Self is a keyword or reserved literal.
  They CAN be used as regular identifiers outside method/type context,
  though this is strongly discouraged.

A.4.7 Integer Literals
  decimal ::= [0-9]+
  hex     ::= 0x[0-9a-fA-F]+
  octal   ::= 0o[0-7]+
  binary  ::= 0b[01]+
  No suffixes. Type is inferred from context or explicit cast.

A.4.8 Floating-Point Literals
  float ::= [0-9]+ "." [0-9]+ ([eE] [+-]? [0-9]+)?
        | [0-9]+ [eE] [+-]? [0-9]+
  No suffixes. Default type is f64 unless context demands f32.

A.4.9 String Literals
  string ::= " ([^"] | \ [nt"\0])* "
  No multi-line strings. Use concatenation or std.builtin.embed_file.
  Type is []const u8.

A.4.10 Character Literals
  char ::= ' ([^'] | \ [nt'\0]) '
  Type is u8. There is no distinct char type.

A.4.11 Operators and Punctuation

  +  -  *  /  %
  == != <  >  <= >=
  =  += -= *= /= %=
  &  |  ^  <<  >>
  && || !  ~
  .  .. -> => :: #
  ( ) [ ] { }
  , ; : ? &

A.4.12 Attribute Syntax
  attribute  ::= "#" "[" attr_body "]"
  attr_body  ::= identifier attr_args?
  attr_args  ::= "(" attr_arg ("," attr_arg)* ")"
  attr_arg   ::= expr

  Canonical Examples:
    #[packed]
    #[align(16)]
    #[extern("C")]
    #[section(".text.boot")]

