C. SEMANTIC NOTES (Grammar Clarifications)

C.1 NO OUTER PARENTHESES IN CONTROL FLOW
    while (true) { }   -- SYNTAX ERROR
    while true { }     -- OK

C.2 SELF PARAMETER FORMS
    Methods may declare self in shorthand or explicit form:
      pub fn len(&const self) -> usize;        // shorthand
      pub fn len(self: &const Vec3) -> usize;  // explicit (equivalent)

    The shorthand is rewritten to explicit form during semantic analysis:
      &const self  =>  self: &const Self
      &self       =>  self: &Self
      self        =>  self: Self

C.3 TRAIT OBJECT-SAFETY RULES (Compiler Enforcement)
    A trait is object-safe (can form &Trait) iff ALL methods:
    - Have no comptime parameters (except implicit self)
    - Have no generic parameters
    - Do not return T by value
    - Have receiver self: &T or self: &const T

C.4 ERROR UNION LAYOUT
    !T is represented as:
      struct { tag: u32, /* padding to alignof(T) */, payload: T }
    Tag 0x00000000 = Success.
    Non-zero = (module_id: u16 << 16) | error_code: u16.
    This yields 65,536 modules max and 65,536 distinct errors per module.

C.5 CASTING RULES
    x as T is allowed for:
    - Integer <-> Integer (checked in debug, wrapping in release)
    - Integer <-> Float
    - Pointer <-> Pointer (in unsafe only)
    - Pointer <-> Integer (in unsafe only)
    - Enum <-> Integer

C.6 DEFER / ERRDEFER SCOPE
    - defer executes its expression when the enclosing BLOCK exits (not function).
    - errdefer executes its block when the enclosing FUNCTION exits via error.
    - Multiple defers in the same block execute in LIFO order.
    - errdefer blocks execute in LIFO order before the error is propagated.

C.7 UNSAFE CONTEXTS
    The following require unsafe { ... } or an unsafe fn:
    - Dereferencing raw pointers (*ptr)
    - Pointer arithmetic
    - Type punning between pointer types
    - Inline assembly
    - Calling extern C functions without wrappers
    - Coercing *void to typed pointers

C.8 GENERIC CONSTRAINTS
    Syntax: T: type with Copyable, Serializable
    The compiler checks structural implementation BEFORE monomorphization.

C.9 UNDEFINED SEMANTICS
    - undefined represents uninitialized memory. It is not a specific bit pattern.
    - Reading undefined memory in safe code is a safety-checked compile error
      if the compiler can prove the read occurs; otherwise it is a runtime
      panic in debug builds and UB in release-fast builds.
    - undefined is valid only in initialization contexts:
        var x: u32 = undefined;   // OK
        var y: u32 = x + 1;       // ERROR: x is undefined
    - undefined may not be passed to comptime functions or used in
      compile-time constant evaluation.

    - true, false, and null are reserved literal tokens, not keywords.
    - They are recognized by the lexer as dedicated token types.
    - They cannot be used as identifiers.

C.10 ATTRIBUTE SEMANTICS
    - Attributes decorate the declaration that immediately follows them.
    - Multiple attributes may be stacked; order is significant only for
      user-defined comptime attributes.
    - Unknown attributes are a compile error unless explicitly permitted by
      a comptime plugin hook.

C.11 PRIMITIVE TYPE NAMES
    - Primitive type names (i8, u32, f64, bool, void, etc.) are reserved
      and cannot be shadowed by user declarations, but they are NOT keywords.
    - They are valid only in type-expression context; using them as values
      is a compile error.

C.12 SELF AND SELF
    - self is a contextual identifier referring to the receiver instance inside
      struct, union, enum, or trait methods. It lexes as an IDENTIFIER token.
    - Self is a contextual identifier referring to the enclosing type within
      a struct, union, enum, trait, or extend definition. It lexes as an
      IDENTIFIER token.
    - In an extend Vec3(T: type) block, Self resolves to Vec3(T).
    - In an extend Vec3(f32) with Printable block, Self resolves to Vec3(f32).
    - Neither is a keyword. Outside method/type context they are ordinary
      identifiers, though shadowing them is strongly discouraged.

C.13 NO BUILT-IN STRING OR CHAR TYPE
    - There is no string keyword, primitive type, or built-in string type.
    - String literals produce []const u8.
    - Null-terminated string literals (future) produce *const [N:0]u8.
    - Growable, formatted, or Unicode-aware string types live in std lib
      (e.g., std.String).
    - There is no char primitive type. Character literals yield u8.

C.14 RESERVED LITERAL TOKENS
    - true, false, and null are reserved literal tokens, not keywords.
    - They are recognized by the lexer as dedicated token types.
    - They cannot be used as identifiers.

C.15 BLOCK TRAILING EXPRESSIONS
    - A block may end with a trailing expression (no semicolon).
    - If present, the trailing expression is the value of the block.
    - If absent, the block evaluates to void.
    - This applies to all block contexts: function bodies, if/else arms,
      while bodies, unsafe blocks, etc.

    Example:
      const x = if cond {
        42    // trailing expression: block value is 42
      } else {
        0
      };

C.16 MATCH ARM TRAILING COMMA
    - The trailing comma after the last match arm is OPTIONAL.
    - It is permitted for cleaner diffs and generated code.

C.17 TRY IN EXPRESSION POSITION
    - try expr is a valid expression at any precedence level.
    - It may appear in statement position, assignment, or nested expressions.

    Example:
      const x = try might_fail();          // OK
      const y = (try a()) + (try b());     // OK

C.18 GENERIC INSTANTIATION SYNTAX
    - Generic types are instantiated with function-call syntax: Vec3(f32).
    - No angle brackets <> are used for generics.
    - This avoids ambiguity with comparison operators and keeps syntax uniform.

C.19 ARRAY LITERAL REPEAT-FILL
    - [N]T{ val, } fills all N elements with val.
    - [N]T{ a, b, c } requires exactly N elements.
    - []T{ a, b, c } infers length from initializer count.

C.20 BITFIELD PACKING
    - Fields of type u1, u2, u3, etc. in a #[packed] struct are
      automatically bit-packed by the compiler.
    - Byte-aligned fields (u8, u16, u32, u64, u128, usize, isize, bool)
      maintain normal alignment even inside #[packed].
    - Mixed bitfields and byte-aligned fields in the same struct are allowed;
      the compiler inserts padding to align byte-sized fields.
    - Bitfield widths u1-u7 are valid ONLY inside #[packed] structs.
      Using them elsewhere is a compile error.

    Example:
      #[packed]
      struct StatusReg {
        ready:    u1,
        error:    u1,
        reserved: u6,   // packed into first byte
        status:  u16,  // starts at byte offset 1, aligned
      }

C.21 POINTER DEREFERENCE SYNTAX
    - *ptr is the only dereference syntax. It requires an unsafe context.
    - There is NO ptr.* sugar.
    - Auto-dereference applies only to &T and &const T, not *T.
    - To access a field through a raw pointer: (*ptr).field inside unsafe.

C.22 EXTERN FUNCTION BODIES
    - Functions marked with #[extern("C")] may omit their body and end with ";".
    - If a body is provided, it is treated as a wrapper around the extern call.
    - Extern functions without a body have no Raya implementation; linkage is
      resolved by the platform linker.

================================================================================
D. TYPE SYSTEM & POINTER TAXONOMY

D.1 REFERENCES (&T, &const T, ?&T)
    - Non-null, address-aligned memory locations.
    - Auto-dereferencing on field access and method call.
    - NO pointer arithmetic permitted.
    - Rebindable: var r: &i32 = &x; r = &y; // Legal
    - Aliasing: Multiple &T to the same address is PERMITTED. The programmer is
      responsible for synchronization. This is NOT a compiler error in Path A.
      [LEAD ENG NOTE: This is an intentional design choice for systems
       programming flexibility, but it voids Rust-style borrow-checker guarantees.
       Teams must enforce aliasing discipline via code review and sanitizers.]
    - Struct fields: A struct MAY contain &T or []T fields. The compiler tracks
      NOTHING about the referent's lifetime. Dangling references are programmer
      bugs, not compile-time errors.

    &T      : Mutable reference. Cannot be null.
    &const T: Immutable reference. Cannot be null. Multiple &const T allowed.
    ?&T     : Optional reference. Null-pointer optimized: sizeof(?&T) == sizeof(&T).
              The null bit is the zero address. No extra tag.

D.2 SLICES ([]T, []const T)
    - Fat pointer: struct { ptr: *T, len: usize } in target native endianness.
    - Bounds checking on index: slice[i] checks 0 <= i < len.
    - Panic/abort in Debug and ReleaseSafe modes. UB in ReleaseFast.
    - Sub-slicing: slice[start..end] constructs new slice with bounds check.
    - Empty slice is valid: ptr may be undefined, len == 0.

D.3 RAW POINTERS (*T, *const T, ?*T)
    - C-style unrestricted access.
    - Permits arbitrary pointer arithmetic (scaled by sizeof(T)).
    - Explicit dereference: *ptr (requires unsafe context).
    - Casting: &T coerces to *T implicitly in unsafe blocks.
    *T to &T requires explicit unsafe assertion of validity.

D.4 FIXED ARRAYS ([N]T)
    - Stack-allocated contiguous sequence.
    - Implicitly coerces to []T: buf -> buf[0..N]
    - Implicitly coerces to []const T.

D.5 OPAQUE POINTERS (*void, *const void) — FFI ONLY
    - Valid only in #[extern("C")] contexts and unsafe blocks.
    - Coerce to/from any *T or *const T in unsafe contexts.
    - Cannot be dereferenced (void has no size).

D.6 COERCION MATRIX

    From -> To              Mechanism
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    [N]T    -> []T          Implicit coercion
    [N]T    -> []const T    Implicit coercion
    &T      -> &const T     Implicit coercion (freezes mutability)
    &T      -> *T           Implicit coercion inside unsafe only
    *T      -> &T           Explicit unsafe assertion required
    []T     -> *T           Explicit: slice.ptr
    *void   -> *T           Explicit unsafe ptr_cast only
    T       -> !T           Implicit on return (wraps success tag)
    !T      -> T            Explicit: try (propagates), or else |err| (unwraps)

