================================================================================
F. COMPTIME EXECUTION MODEL (Bytecode Subset)

The comptime interpreter is a stack-based bytecode VM with the following
instruction set. This is the MINIMUM viable set for v1.0.

F.1 VALUE TYPES IN THE INTERPRETER

- Integer (i128) — for all integer math
- Float (f128) — for all float math
- Bool
- String (heap-allocated, reference-counted)
- Type (ComptimeType handle)
- Descriptor (DeclDescriptor handle)
- Expr (AST node handle)
- Stmt (AST node handle)
- Pointer (opaque heap pointer for composite values)
- Void

F.2 INSTRUCTION SET (v1.0 Minimum)

Opcode        Operands         Description
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NOP           —                No operation
PUSH_INT      imm: i128        Push integer literal
PUSH_FLT      imm: f128        Push float literal
PUSH_STR      idx: u32         Push string from constant pool
PUSH_TYP      idx: u32         Push type from type table
PUSH_BOOL     imm: u8          Push true (1) or false (0)
PUSH_NULL     —                Push null
DUP           —                Duplicate top of stack
POP           —                Pop and discard top of stack
LD_LOCAL      idx: u16         Load local variable
ST_LOCAL      idx: u16         Store to local variable
LD_GLOBAL     idx: u16         Load global comptime variable
ST_GLOBAL     idx: u16         Store to global comptime variable
ADD_I         —                Integer addition
SUB_I         —                Integer subtraction
MUL_I         —                Integer multiplication
DIV_I         —                Integer division (checked)
MOD_I         —                Integer modulo
ADD_F         —                Float addition
SUB_F         —                Float subtraction
MUL_F         —                Float multiplication
DIV_F         —                Float division
AND           —                Bitwise AND
OR            —                Bitwise OR
XOR           —                Bitwise XOR
SHL           —                Shift left
SHR           —                Shift right (arithmetic)
NOT           —                Bitwise NOT
NEG_I         —                Integer negation
NEG_F         —                Float negation
EQ            —                Equality (any type)
NE            —                Inequality
LT_I          —                Integer less-than
GT_I          —                Integer greater-than
LE_I          —                Integer less-equal
GE_I          —                Integer greater-equal
LT_F          —                Float less-than
GT_F          —                Float greater-than
LE_F          —                Float less-equal
GE_F          —                Float greater-equal
JMP           offset: i32      Unconditional jump
JZ            offset: i32      Jump if top-of-stack is false/zero
CALL          idx: u32         Call comptime function
RET           —                Return from function
CALL_BUILTIN  idx: u16         Call compiler builtin (size_of, etc.)
MAKE_ARRAY    len: u32         Pop N values, make array
MAKE_STRUCT   idx: u32         Pop N values, make struct (layout from table)
INDEX_ARRAY   —                Pop index, pop array, push element
INDEX_STRUCT  field: u16       Pop struct, push field
FIELD_ACCESS  name: u32        Pop object, push field by name
CONCAT_STR    —                Pop two strings, push concatenation
CAST_INT      target_bits: u8   Pop value, cast to integer type
CAST_FLT      target_bits: u8   Pop value, cast to float type
HALT          —                Terminate execution

F.3 EXECUTION LIMITS (Enforced)

- Max instructions per evaluation: 10^8
- Max heap memory: 2GB
- Max recursion depth: 1024
- Max string length: 1MB
- Max array length: 10^6 elements

F.4 HOST INTERFACE (Compiler Callbacks)

builtin_size_of(T: ComptimeType) -> usize
builtin_align_of(T: ComptimeType) -> usize
builtin_offset_of(T: ComptimeType, field: []const u8) -> usize
builtin_type_name(T: ComptimeType) -> []const u8
builtin_compile_error(msg: []const u8) -> noreturn
builtin_add_method(desc: DeclDescriptor, method: FnDecl) -> DeclDescriptor
builtin_add_trait_impl(desc: DeclDescriptor, trait: []const u8, for_type: ComptimeType, methods: []FnDecl) -> DeclDescriptor

================================================================================
G. FAT-POINTER ABI LAYOUT (Complete)

G.1 MEMORY LAYOUT

A trait object &Trait is a fat pointer with the following layout:

  ┌─────────────────┬─────────────────┐
  │   data: *void   │  vtable: *void  │
  │   (ptr size)    │   (ptr size)    │
  └─────────────────┴─────────────────┘

On 64-bit targets: 16 bytes total (8 + 8).
On 32-bit targets: 8 bytes total (4 + 4).

G.2 VTABLE LAYOUT

The vtable pointed to by the fat pointer has the following layout:

  struct VTable {
      type_id: usize,          // unique type identifier for runtime type checks
      drop_fn: ?*void,         // destructor, null if no cleanup needed
      method_0: *void,         // first trait method
      method_1: *void,         // second trait method
      ...
      method_N: *void,         // Nth trait method (order = declaration order)
  };

- type_id is a compiler-generated hash of the concrete type name.
- drop_fn is called when the trait object goes out of scope (if non-null).
- Methods are laid out in declaration order from the traits block.
- For object-safe traits only (see C.3). Non-object-safe traits cannot form
  fat pointers and attempting to do so is a compile error.

G.3 DYNAMIC DISPATCH

Calling a method on a trait object is lowered to:
  1. Load vtable pointer from fat pointer offset ptr_size.
  2. Index into vtable at offset (2 + method_index) * ptr_size.
  3. Call through function pointer, passing data pointer as first argument.

================================================================================
H. DEFER / ERRDEFER SEMANTICS (Complete)

H.1 DEFER

Syntax: defer <expr>;

- defer registers <expr> to execute when the enclosing BLOCK exits.
- "Enclosing block" means the nearest curly-brace-delimited block:
    function body, if/else arm, while body, for body, unsafe block, etc.
- defer is NOT function-scoped. A defer inside an if arm executes only
  when that if arm exits, not when the function returns.
- Multiple defers in the same block execute in LIFO (last-in-first-out) order.
- defer expressions are evaluated at registration time (eager evaluation of
  the expression itself), but the side effects of that evaluation execute
  at block exit. [CRITICAL CLARIFICATION]

Example:
    fn foo() {
        defer print("A");
        if cond {
            defer print("B");
            // prints "B" when this if-arm exits, then continues
        }
        // prints "A" when function body exits
    }

H.2 ERRDEFER

Syntax: errdefer { <block> }

- errdefer registers <block> to execute when the enclosing FUNCTION exits
  via an error path (try propagating an error, or explicit error return).
- errdefer is FUNCTION-scoped, unlike defer which is BLOCK-scoped.
- errdefer blocks execute in LIFO order BEFORE the error is propagated
  to the caller.
- errdefer does NOT execute on normal (non-error) return paths.
- If multiple errdefers are registered, all execute before propagation.

Example:
    fn open_resource() -> !Handle {
        const fd = try open_file("/tmp/data");
        errdefer { close_file(fd); }

        const handle = try wrap_handle(fd);
        errdefer { release_handle(handle); }

        return handle;   // errdefers do NOT execute
        // If wrap_handle fails, first errdefer executes, then error propagates
    }

H.3 INTERACTION WITH PANIC / NORETURN

- defer blocks execute on normal block exit AND on panic paths.
- errdefer blocks execute only on error-propagation paths, NOT on panic.
- noreturn functions (e.g., panic) do not execute defers in the current
  block because control never reaches the block exit.

H.4 COMPTIME DEFER

- defer and errdefer are permitted inside comptime functions.
- They execute during comptime evaluation with the same scoping rules.

================================================================================
I. MODULE RESOLUTION ALGORITHM (Complete)

I.1 MODULE DECLARATION

A module declaration establishes the root namespace for the current file:
    module mylib.submodule;

- The module name must match the file's path relative to the project root,
  with path separators replaced by dots.
- A file without a module declaration is assigned an anonymous module
  and cannot be imported by name.

I.2 IMPORT RESOLUTION

import statements are resolved in the following order:

1. Builtin modules (std.io, std.mem, std.builtin, etc.)
   - Resolved against the compiler's bundled standard library.

2. Project modules
   - The module path is mapped to a file path by replacing dots with
     platform-appropriate path separators and appending `.ray`.
   - Example: `import mylib.utils` -> `mylib/utils.ray`

3. External packages (future)
   - Resolved via a package manifest (ray.toml or similar).

I.3 VISIBILITY RULES

- Symbols are private by default.
- `pub` makes a symbol visible to importers.
- `pub` on a struct field makes that field accessible outside the module.
- `pub` on a trait method makes that method part of the public interface.
- Items inside an extend block inherit the visibility of the extend
  declaration itself, NOT the visibility of the underlying type.

I.4 CIRCULAR IMPORT DETECTION

- Circular imports are detected at compile time.
- A cycle is a compile error unless all edges in the cycle are `pub`
  declarations and no comptime code in the cycle depends on fully
  resolved types from another module in the cycle.
- [FUTURE] Weak imports for breaking cycles in interface-only modules.

I.5 NAME COLLISION RESOLUTION

- Within a module, names must be unique per namespace (types, values, traits).
- A type and a value may share a name (they live in different namespaces).
- Import aliases (`import std.mem as memory`) shadow the original name
  within the importing module only.

================================================================================
J. ERROR SET TYPE SPECIFICATION

J.1 ERROR SET BASICS

An error set is an implicit type created by the compiler for each module.
It enumerates all error values that can be produced within that module.

Error values are not user-declared. They are created by the compiler from:
- Explicit error return values in the source (future: `return error.OutOfMemory`)
- For v0.7.x, error unions (!T) use a numeric tag space (see C.4).

J.2 ERROR UNION TYPE

!T is the error union type. It can hold either:
- A success value of type T
- An error value (represented as a non-zero u32 tag)

J.3 ERROR PROPAGATION

try expr:
- If expr evaluates to a success value, unwrap it to T.
- If expr evaluates to an error, immediately return that error from the
  current function (wrapping it in the caller's error union if necessary).

expr else |err| block:
- If expr evaluates to a success value, the whole expression has type T.
- If expr evaluates to an error, bind the error code to `err` and execute
  block. The block's trailing expression must have type T.

J.4 ERROR TAG NAMESPACE

- Tag 0x00000000: Success
- Tag 0x00000001 - 0x0000FFFF: Reserved for compiler/runtime errors
- Tag 0x00010000 - 0xFFFFFFFF: Module-specific errors
  - High 16 bits: module_id (assigned by the compiler's module table)
  - Low 16 bits: error_code (per-module sequential assignment)

================================================================================
K. MEMORY MODEL & SAFETY CONTRACT

K.1 STACK AND HEAP

- All local variables are stack-allocated by default.
- Heap allocation is explicit via std.mem functions (e.g., std.mem.alloc).
- There is no garbage collector. Memory management is manual.
- defer is the primary tool for ensuring cleanup.

K.2 SAFETY CONTRACT

Path A makes the following safety guarantees in SAFE code (non-unsafe):

GUARANTEED (compiler-enforced):
- Bounds checking on slice and array indexing.
- Null checking on optional dereference.
- Integer overflow checking in debug builds.
- Type safety: no implicit casts except where explicitly permitted.

NOT GUARANTEED (programmer responsibility):
- Lifetime safety of references (&T) and slices.
- No dangling pointers.
- No data races (mutable aliasing is permitted).
- No use-after-free.

K.3 UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR (UB) CATEGORIES

The following are UB in release-fast builds and panics in debug builds:
- Out-of-bounds slice/array access
- Dereferencing null optional references
- Integer overflow (signed)
- Reading undefined memory (when compiler cannot prove it)

The following are ALWAYS UB (no checks possible):
- Dereferencing dangling raw pointers
- Data races on non-atomic shared mutable state
- Violating the FFI ABI contract

K.4 RECOMMENDED SANITIZER STACK

For production builds, the lead engineering team recommends:
- AddressSanitizer (ASan) for memory safety testing
- ThreadSanitizer (TSan) for race detection
- UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan) for UB catching
- These are enabled via compiler flags, not language features.

